reviewer_feedback.description:
The regex `[^\S
]+` on individual lines (which have already been split on `
`) will never see a `
` character. The `[^\S
]` exclusion is harmless but unnecessary — `[ 	]+` or `\s+` (since newlines are already split out) would be equivalent and clearer.

arbiter_feedback.description:
The list comprehension on line 18 applies `re.sub` followed by `.strip()` to every line individually. The `.strip()` call after `re.sub(r'[^\S
]+', ' ', line)` can only remove a single leading/trailing space (since all runs of whitespace are already collapsed to one space). It would be slightly cleaner and marginally more efficient to use `re.sub(r'^[^\S
]+|[^\S
]+$', '', ...)` or simply trim before the sub. This is purely a style/micro-optimisation note.
